<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Birsay</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Birsay is a parish and village in the north-west of Mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. The tidal island of the Brough of Birsay has remains of Pictish and Norse settlement, and in the 11th century Earl <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thorfinn-the-mighty">Thorfinn the Mighty</a> made Birsay his seat and built a minster there, the first seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of Orkney. The ruined Earl's Palace dates from the 16th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/birsay.jpg" alt="Birsay" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Comloquoy · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brough_of_Birsay.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Birsay, in Old Norse Birgisherað, lies in the north-west corner of the Mainland of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a>. Almost all the land of the parish is farmed, chiefly as grassland for beef <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>. Its two best-known monuments, the prehistoric and Norse settlements on the tidal island of the Brough of Birsay and the ruined Earl's Palace on the Mainland opposite, at the northern end of the village, are maintained by Historic Scotland.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Architectural fragments and excavation of the foundations of St Magnus Church suggest that it stands on the site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christchurch">Christchurch</a>, the first cathedral of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a> in the 11th and 12th centuries, founded by Earl Thorfinn the Mighty, who died around 1065, after his return from a pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. The seat of the diocese moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-magnus-cathedral">St Magnus Cathedral</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kirkwall">Kirkwall</a> in the later 12th century, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishops</a> of Orkney kept a residence at Birsay, known as Mons Bellus, into late medieval times.</p><p>The Earl's Palace was built in the late 16th century by Robert Stewart, 1st Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a>, and the date 1574 was once carved above its south-facing entrance. It had four ranges round an open courtyard with small corner towers, a form unusual in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> at that date and without precedent in the north, and it was set among walled gardens, an archery range and a bowling green. With the Earl's Palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kirkwall">Kirkwall</a> and Scalloway Castle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shetland">Shetland</a> it was one of the main residences of the Stewart Earls of Orkney, and it has stood uninhabited since the late 17th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>St Magnus Church now serves the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-scotland">Church of Scotland</a> parish of Birsay and Harray, and the bridge nearby may also be medieval in origin. Samuel Trail, minister of the parish from 1844 to 1868, became Moderator of the General Assembly of the Church of Scotland in 1874.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Birsay” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Thorfinn the Mighty, in the 11th century, ruled much of northern Scotland and built a minster at <b>Birsay</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/earldom-of-orkney">Earldom of Orkney</a></li></ul></section>
